1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:01,910
Ten forty-seven on a Friday night.

2
00:00:01,910 --> 00:00:06,280
You finish dinner with friends at your regular spot, and the check comes to 10 million dong.

3
00:00:06,280 --> 00:00:08,130
You tap your credit card on the reader.

4
00:00:08,130 --> 00:00:08,840
One crisp beep.

5
00:00:08,840 --> 00:00:11,310
Your phone buzzes in your pocket: ten million spent.

6
00:00:11,310 --> 00:00:14,680
You take the receipt, pull on your jacket, and walk out into the night.

7
00:00:14,680 --> 00:00:16,580
As far as you're concerned, you're done.

8
00:00:16,580 --> 00:00:20,380
Except at the exact second that reader beeped, not a single dong moved anywhere.

9
00:00:20,380 --> 00:00:22,040
No cash left your checking account.

10
00:00:22,040 --> 00:00:24,650
No cash landed in the restaurant's bank account either.

11
00:00:24,650 --> 00:00:26,830
So tonight, where is that 10 million sleeping?

12
00:00:26,830 --> 00:00:34,780
To track it down, we follow three layers linking five players: the approval messages, the interbank cash transfer, and the accounting books waiting behind the counter.

13
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:39,600
Zoom in on the exact second your card hits the reader.

14
00:00:39,600 --> 00:00:44,260
That ping on your phone does not mean cash walked out of your savings.

15
00:00:44,260 --> 00:00:46,590
You haven't paid a single dong yet.

16
00:00:46,590 --> 00:00:52,780
Your issuing bank simply placed an authorization hold of 10 million dong on your credit line.

17
00:00:52,780 --> 00:01:01,440
Think of it like sticking a Post-it note on your credit limit: this chunk is spoken for, do not let the cardholder spend it twice.

18
00:01:01,440 --> 00:01:06,170
That hold is not a real debt entry on the bank's accounting ledger yet.

19
00:01:06,170 --> 00:01:08,630
Your total loan balance hasn't moved.

20
00:01:08,630 --> 00:01:13,620
You won't pay a dong until your monthly statement falls due weeks from now.

21
00:01:13,620 --> 00:01:20,080
And if your group canceled the check on the spot, your bank would just peel off that sticky note.

22
00:01:20,810 --> 00:01:26,290
In the two seconds after your tap, a quick digital message races across four companies.

23
00:01:26,290 --> 00:01:30,450
The restaurant's terminal pings its own bank, called the acquirer.

24
00:01:30,450 --> 00:01:37,190
The acquirer passes that note across the card network switch to the bank that issued your card, the issuer.

25
00:01:37,190 --> 00:01:44,120
Your issuer runs three fast checks: is the card real, is the limit high enough, and does this look like fraud?

26
00:01:44,120 --> 00:01:49,730
Everything checks out, so a single word flashes back to the restaurant counter: approved.

27
00:01:49,730 --> 00:01:51,560
Notice one crucial rule here.

28
00:01:51,560 --> 00:01:56,160
The card network logo stamped on your card never lends you a single dong.

29
00:01:56,160 --> 00:02:04,040
It sells the messaging rails and the shared rulebook so thousands of banks can trust one another, billing fees to both banks.

30
00:02:04,040 --> 00:02:07,070
The bank lending you money is your issuing bank.

31
00:02:08,590 --> 00:02:09,080
Midnight.

32
00:02:09,080 --> 00:02:12,730
The waiters wipe down the tables and roll down the front shutters.

33
00:02:12,730 --> 00:02:16,990
Pop open the cash register behind the bar: your 10 million dong isn't inside.

34
00:02:16,990 --> 00:02:20,910
Open the restaurant owner's banking app: zero new deposits from dinner.

35
00:02:20,910 --> 00:02:26,160
All the business holds tonight is a commercial promise from its acquiring bank to pay up later.

36
00:02:26,160 --> 00:02:31,020
The restaurant's bookkeeper logs that promise onto the accounting books as a receivable.

37
00:02:31,020 --> 00:02:35,720
Look closely at who owes that money: the acquiring bank owes the restaurant, not you.

38
00:02:35,720 --> 00:02:39,260
Having tapped your card, you owe the diner bill to nobody there.

39
00:02:39,260 --> 00:02:42,910
Debit 1388 receivable ten million; credit 511 revenue ten million.

40
00:02:42,910 --> 00:02:44,070
Two lines that match.

41
00:02:44,070 --> 00:02:51,420
So tonight, your 10 million sleeps inside a single ledger line — which is why smaller spots often prefer direct transfers over cards.

42
00:02:52,290 --> 00:02:58,670
Before locking up, the restaurant manager hits one final button on the card machine: batch close.

43
00:02:58,670 --> 00:03:09,730
The acquiring bank sweeps every approved tap from the shift into one digital list, called a presentment file, and routes it across the card network back to each issuer.

44
00:03:09,730 --> 00:03:11,180
This step is clearing.

45
00:03:11,180 --> 00:03:16,770
Both banks cross-check approval codes, verify totals, and calculate exact fee splits.

46
00:03:16,770 --> 00:03:25,790
Only when that clearing line hits your issuing bank does 10 million dong officially post onto your card debt and lift the temporary hold.

47
00:03:25,790 --> 00:03:32,960
That explains why your statement lists two dates: transaction date for dinner, and posting date for clearing.

48
00:03:32,960 --> 00:03:36,510
Even now, nothing but data has traveled between banks.

49
00:03:36,510 --> 00:03:39,080
Real money hasn't shifted a millimeter.

50
00:03:40,120 --> 00:03:47,130
Two business days later — T plus 2 in banking shorthand — actual money finally runs between banks.

51
00:03:47,130 --> 00:03:50,280
Watch who puts real cash on the table first.

52
00:03:50,280 --> 00:03:52,570
Not you, and not the restaurant.

53
00:03:52,570 --> 00:03:55,860
Your issuing bank advances the money up front.

54
00:03:55,860 --> 00:04:04,090
In this example, it sends 9.85 million dong to the acquiring bank, having already kept 150,000 dong as its own cut.

55
00:04:04,090 --> 00:04:11,170
The acquiring bank takes its slice and drops 9,780,000 dong into the restaurant's checking account.

56
00:04:11,170 --> 00:04:20,540
Over on the restaurant's books, last night's receivable drops to zero: checking rises 9,780,000, and 220,000 hits selling expenses.

57
00:04:20,540 --> 00:04:22,760
The promise became real cash...

58
00:04:22,760 --> 00:04:24,900
short by exactly the card fee.

59
00:04:25,750 --> 00:04:28,130
So where did that missing 220,000 dong go?

60
00:04:28,130 --> 00:04:32,030
That cut is the merchant discount rate, or MDR, modeled here at 2.2%.

61
00:04:32,030 --> 00:04:34,010
Three firms split every dong of it.

62
00:04:34,010 --> 00:04:39,780
The biggest piece — roughly 150,000 dong, or 1.5% — goes straight to your issuing bank as interchange.

63
00:04:39,780 --> 00:04:43,850
That is why your bank wired 9.85 million instead of the full 10 million.

64
00:04:43,850 --> 00:04:49,220
Another small slice, say 10,000 dong, pays the card network switch to route all those messages.

65
00:04:49,220 --> 00:04:55,610
The remaining 60,000 dong stays with the acquiring bank for maintaining terminals and underwriting merchant risk.

66
00:04:55,610 --> 00:05:00,590
All three companies made one tap happen, and all three got paid out of one dinner check.

67
00:05:00,590 --> 00:05:02,950
Real rates vary by card tier and contract.

68
00:05:03,800 --> 00:05:10,260
One detail hides this money trail in daily life: banks never settle restaurant receipts one by one.

69
00:05:10,260 --> 00:05:19,320
Suppose on that same afternoon, customers carrying cards issued by the restaurant's bank spent 3 million dong at shops served by your bank.

70
00:05:19,320 --> 00:05:24,210
After 1.5% interchange (45,000 dong), their bank owes yours 2,955,000 dong.

71
00:05:24,210 --> 00:05:31,450
Instead of firing separate wires in both directions, the card network nets every bill against every other bill.

72
00:05:31,450 --> 00:05:38,620
In this two-bank example, your bank wires just the 6,895,000 dong difference into the central settlement pool.

73
00:05:38,620 --> 00:05:45,530
In real banking, hundreds of thousands of swipes collapse into one single net transfer per bank every day.

74
00:05:45,530 --> 00:05:50,150
Your 10 million dinner bill dissolves into a sea of settlement traffic.

75
00:05:51,820 --> 00:05:54,440
Weeks pass, and your monthly statement lands.

76
00:05:54,440 --> 00:05:59,780
Before the payment deadline, you transfer 10 million dong from checking to pay off the card.

77
00:05:59,780 --> 00:06:04,480
Only now does real cash leave your bank account, dropping your debt back to zero.

78
00:06:04,480 --> 00:06:09,130
Then you spot a pleasant bonus on the page: 100,000 dong in 1% cashback rewards.

79
00:06:09,130 --> 00:06:12,030
That bonus is not free money from a generous bank.

80
00:06:12,030 --> 00:06:21,440
One main source is the 150,000 dong interchange fee collected from the restaurant's bill weeks ago, topped up by annual fees and interest from folks who pay late.

81
00:06:21,440 --> 00:06:29,630
On this ticket alone, your bank brings in 150,000 and hands you back 100,000, leaving 50,000 to cover network fees and upfront capital costs.

82
00:06:29,630 --> 00:06:36,650
And since restaurants fold card fees into menu prices, part of your cashback is your own dinner money finishing a circle.

83
00:06:38,390 --> 00:06:41,150
What happens when someone makes a mistake?

84
00:06:41,150 --> 00:06:45,750
Payment networks run three backup paths, split by who pulls the lever.

85
00:06:45,750 --> 00:06:53,300
If the server accidentally taps in 100 million and cancels during the shift before batch close, that is a reversal.

86
00:06:53,300 --> 00:06:57,830
The hold vanishes instantly, zero money shifts, and nobody pays fees.

87
00:06:57,830 --> 00:07:07,090
If you settle a wrong order the next day, the restaurant sends a refund: a fresh credit record flows backward across the next clearing batch.

88
00:07:07,090 --> 00:07:14,310
But if you spot a fraudulent double charge and the restaurant stalls, you call your bank to file a chargeback.

89
00:07:14,310 --> 00:07:23,700
Here the cardholder starts the fight, and the acquiring bank yanks 10 million straight back from the restaurant while both sides show receipts.

90
00:07:23,700 --> 00:07:26,590
Even settled money is never completely safe.

91
00:07:27,950 --> 00:07:34,810
So tonight, after you tap for a 10 million dong dinner, your money sleeps in a restaurant receivable line on a general ledger.

92
00:07:34,810 --> 00:07:42,710
Two business days later, in this example, your issuing bank puts up the cash, the restaurant clears 9,780,000, and three middlemen split 220,000.

93
00:07:42,710 --> 00:07:46,310
Weeks later, you pay off the bill and collect 100,000 in cashback.

94
00:07:46,310 --> 00:07:50,230
Card taps feel effortless and free, yet somebody always covers the toll.

95
00:07:50,230 --> 00:07:53,880
That toll just sits behind the counter rather than on your receipt.

96
00:07:53,880 --> 00:07:59,330
Next time you pull out your card, ask one quick question: who already priced that fee into the dish?
